<article class="env-remark" id="016L">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/016L">Remark <span data-tag="016L">14.5.3</span>.</a> By abuse of notation we sometimes write $\delta _ i : U_{n - 1} \to U_ n$ instead of $\delta ^ n_ i$, and similarly for $\sigma _ i : U_{n + 1} \to U_ n$. The relations among the morphisms $\delta ^ n_ i$ and $\sigma ^ n_ i$ may be expressed as follows: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $i < j$, then $\delta _ j \circ \delta _ i = \delta _ i \circ \delta _{j - 1}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $i < j$, then $\sigma _ j \circ \delta _ i = \delta _ i \circ \sigma _{j - 1}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We have $\text{id} = \sigma _ j \circ \delta _ j = \sigma _ j \circ \delta _{j + 1}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $i > j + 1$, then $\sigma _ j \circ \delta _ i = \delta _{i - 1} \circ \sigma _ j$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $i \leq j$, then $\sigma _ j \circ \sigma _ i = \sigma _ i \circ \sigma _{j + 1}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> This means that whenever the compositions on both the left and the right are defined then the corresponding equality should hold. </p>
</article>